  TaggingRules-><|
   "ID" -> "vhwllfq65", "Name" -> "DocumentationSearcher", "DisplayName" -> 
    "Documentation Searcher", "FormattingFunction" -> 
    Wolfram`Chatbook`Tools`Private`documentationSearchFormatter, "ToolCall" -> 
    "/doc_search\nWolfram Language root condition polynomial \.10rho BDF \
method numerical stability roots simplify finding roots\n/exec\nRESULT\n# \
NRoots\npaclet:ref/NRoots#22636\n\nNRoots[lhs == rhs, var] yields a \
disjunction of equations which represent numerical approximations to the \
roots of a polynomial equation.\n\n## Details and Options\n\n* ``NRoots`` \
gives several identical equations when roots with multiplicity greater than \
one occur.\n\n* ``NRoots`` has the following options:\n\n|                |   \
        |                                      |\n| -------------- | \
--------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| MaxIterations  | \
Automatic | maximum number of iterations to use  |\n| Method         | \
Automatic | method to use                        |\n| PrecisionGoal  | \
Automatic | the precision sought                 |\n| StepMonitor    | None   \
   | expression to evaluate at each step  |\n\n* Possible settings for the \
``Method`` option include: ``\"Aberth\"``, ``\"CompanionMatrix\"``, and \
``\"JenkinsTraub\"``.\n\n======\n\n# NRoots > Examples > Options > Method\n\
paclet:ref/NRoots#597745998\n\nUse the ``\"Aberth\"`` method, which \
simultaneously approximates all the roots of a univariate polynomial and \
converges cubically in general (but linearly at multiple zeros):\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= Block[{it = 0}, NRoots[z ^ 5 - 10z ^ 4 + 43z ^ 3 - 104z ^ 2 + 150z - \
100 == 0, z, Method -> \"Aberth\", StepMonitor :> Print[\"Iteration: \", \
++it, \", roots:\", Chop[N[z]]]]]\n\nDuring evaluation of In[1]:=\n\
\"Iteration: \"1\", roots:\"{Complex[0.4499646220416188, \
-0.4919108489913469], \n Complex[0.1458910930532221, 1.4349101951891194], \
Complex[2.2413246459073264, 0.9089071956336795], \n \
Complex[-1.3605135801244272, 6.414748849198775], Complex[1.3605135801244264, \
-6.414748849198775]}\n\nDuring evaluation of In[1]:=\n\"Iteration: \"2\", \
roots:\"{1.9999999999999984, Complex[1., 2.], \n Complex[0.9999999999999999, \
-2.0000000000000004], Complex[2.9999999999999982, \n  -0.9999999999999968], \
Complex[3.0000000000000004, 0.9999999999999964]}\n\nOut[1]= z == 1.\
\[VeryThinSpace] - 2. I || z == 1.\[VeryThinSpace] + 2. I || z == 2. || z == \
3.\[VeryThinSpace] - 1. I || z == 3.\[VeryThinSpace] + 1. I\n```\n\n======\n\n\
# NRoots > Examples > Properties & Relations > 3\npaclet:ref/NRoots#430827256\
\n\nCompare with the numerical roots returned by ``NSolve`` :\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= NSolve[1 + 2 x + 3 x ^ 2 + 4 x ^ 3 == 0, x]\n\nOut[1]= {{x -> \
-0.60583}, {x -> -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I}, {x -> -0.0720852 + 0.638327 I}}\n\
```\n\n======\n\n# NRoots > Examples > Scope\npaclet:ref/NRoots#1470975036\n\n\
Find the numerical roots of a polynomial with only real roots:\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= NRoots[x ^ 3 - 5x ^ 2 + 6x - 1 == 0, x]\n\nOut[1]= x == 0.198062 || x \
== 1.55496 || x == 3.24698\n```\n\nFind the numerical roots of a polynomial \
with real and complex roots:\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= NRoots[x ^ 3 - x ^ 2 - x - 1 \
== 0, x]\n\nOut[2]= x == -0.419643 - 0.606291 I || x == -0.419643 + 0.606291 \
I || x == 1.83929\n```\n\nFind the numerical roots of a polynomial with \
multiple roots:\n\n```wl\nIn[3]:= NRoots[(x - 1)(x - 2)^2(x - 3)^3 == 0, x]\n\
\nOut[3]= x == 1. || x == 2. || x == 2. || x == 3. || x == 3. || x == 3.\n```\
\n\n======\n\n# Numerical Operations on Functions > Numerical Root Finding\n\
paclet:tutorial/NumericalOperationsOnFunctions#28899\n\n``NSolve`` gives you \
a general way to find numerical approximations to the solutions of polynomial \
equations. Finding numerical solutions to more general equations, however, \
can be much more difficult, as discussed in [\"Equations in One \
Variable\"](paclet:tutorial/ManipulatingEquationsAndInequalities#16840). \
``FindRoot`` gives you a way to search for a numerical root of a function or \
a numerical solution to an arbitrary equation, or set of equations.\n\n|      \
                                            |                                 \
                                                 |\n| \
------------------------------------------------ | \
------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
-- |\n| FindRoot[f, {x, x0}]                             | search for a \
numerical root of f, starting with x = x0                           |\n| \
FindRoot[lhs == rhs, {x, x0}]                    | search for a numerical \
solution to the equation lhs == rhs, starting with x = x0 |\n| FindRoot[f1, \
f2, \[Ellipsis], {{x, x0}, {y, y0}, \[Ellipsis]}]       |                     \
                                                             |\n|             \
                                     | search for a simultaneous numerical \
root of all the fi                           |\n| FindRoot[{eqn1, eqn2, \
\[Ellipsis]}, {{x, x0}, {y, y0}, \[Ellipsis]}] |                              \
                                                    |\n|                      \
                            | search for a numerical solution to the \
simultaneous equations eqni               |\n\nNumerical root finding.\n\nThe \
curves for $$\\cos (x)$$ and $$x$$ intersect at one point:\n\n```wl\nIn[53]:= \
Plot[{Cos[x], x}, {x, -1, 1}]\n\nOut[53]= <image removed>\n```\n\nThis finds \
a numerical approximation to the value of $$x$$ at which the intersection \
occurs. The ``0`` tells ``FindRoot`` what value of $$x$$ to try first:\n\n\
```wl\nIn[54]:= FindRoot[Cos[x] == x, {x, 0}]\n\nOut[54]= {x -> 0.739085}\n\
```\n\nIn trying to find a solution to your equation, ``FindRoot`` starts at \
the point you specify, and then progressively tries to get closer and closer \
to a solution. Even if your equations have several solutions, ``FindRoot`` \
always returns the first solution it finds. Which solution this is will \
depend on what starting point you chose. If you start sufficiently close to a \
particular solution, ``FindRoot`` will usually return that solution.\n\nThe \
function $$\\sin (x)$$ has an infinite number of roots of the form $$x=n \
\\pi$$. If you start sufficiently close to a particular root, ``FindRoot`` \
will give you that root:\n\n```wl\nIn[55]:= FindRoot[Sin[x], {x, 3}]\n\n\
Out[55]= {x -> 3.14159}\n```\n\nIf you start with $$x=6$$, you get a \
numerical approximation to the root $$x=2\\pi$$ :\n\n```wl\nIn[56]:= \
FindRoot[Sin[x], {x, 6}]\n\nOut[56]= {x -> 6.28319}\n```\n\nIf you want \
``FindRoot`` to search for complex solutions, then you have to give a complex \
starting value:\n\n```wl\nIn[57]:= FindRoot[Sin[x] == 2, {x, I}]\n\nOut[57]= \
{x -> 1.5708 + 1.31696 I}\n```\n\nThis finds a zero of the Riemann zeta \
function:\n\n```wl\nIn[58]:= FindRoot[Zeta[1 / 2 + I t], {t, 12}]\n\nOut[58]= \
{t -> 14.1347 - 2.5483929136770888`*^-15 I}\n```\n\nThis finds a solution to \
a set of simultaneous equations:\n\n```wl\nIn[59]:= FindRoot[{Sin[x] == \
Cos[y], x + y == 1}, {{x, 1}, {y, 1}}]\n\nOut[59]= {x -> -1.85619, y -> \
2.85619}\n```\n\nThe variables used by ``FindRoot`` can have values that are \
lists. This allows you to find roots of functions that take vectors as \
arguments.\n\nThis is a way to solve a linear equation for the variable $$x$$ \
:\n\n```wl\nIn[60]:= FindRoot[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}.x == {5, 6}, {x, {1, 1}}]\n\n\
Out[60]= {x -> {-4., 4.5}}\n```\n\nThis finds a normalized eigenvector $$x$$ \
and eigenvalue $$a$$ :\n\n```wl\nIn[61]:= FindRoot[{{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}.x == a \
x, x.x == 1}, {{x, {1, 1}}, {a, 1}}]\n\nOut[61]= {x -> {0.415974, 0.909377}, \
a -> 5.37228}\n```\n\n======\n\n# NRoots > Examples > Basic Examples\n\
paclet:ref/NRoots#66905662\n\nNumerically find the roots of a polynomial:\n\n\
```wl\nIn[1]:= NRoots[1 + 2x + 3x ^ 2 + 4x ^ 3 == 0, x]\n\nOut[1]= x == \
-0.60583 || x == -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I || x == -0.0720852 + 0.638327 I\n```\
\n\nChange the result to rules:\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= {ToRules[%]}\n\nOut[2]= {{x \
-> -0.60583}, {x -> -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I}, {x -> -0.0720852 + 0.638327 I}}\
\n```\n\nTest the roots:\n\n```wl\nIn[3]:= 1 + 2x + 3x ^ 2 + 4x ^ 3 /. %\n\n\
Out[3]= {1.1102230246251565`*^-16, 1.1102230246251565`*^-16 + 0. I, \
1.1102230246251565`*^-16 + 0. I}\n```\n\n======\n\n# NRoots > Examples > \
Properties & Relations\npaclet:ref/NRoots#1452787437\n\nReturn numerical \
roots using ``NRoots`` :\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:= NRoots[1 + 2 x + 3 x ^ 2 + 4 x ^ 3 \
== 0, x]\n\nOut[1]= x == -0.60583 || x == -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I || x == \
-0.0720852 + 0.638327 I\n\nIn[2]:= {ToRules[%]}\n\nOut[2]= {{x -> -0.60583}, \
{x -> -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I}, {x -> -0.0720852 + 0.638327 I}}\n```\n\n\
======\n\n# FindRoot > Examples > Basic Examples > 2\n\
paclet:ref/FindRoot#24146\n\nFind a solution to $$\\cos (x)=x$$ near $$x=0$$ \
:\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:= FindRoot[Cos[x] == x, {x, 0}]\n\nOut[1]= {x -> 0.739085}\n\
```\n\n======\n\n# WFR > NumericalMethodFindRoot > Description\n\
https://resources.wolframcloud.com/FunctionRepository/resources/\
NumericalMethodFindRoot#152795533\n\nFind the root of an equation or number \
using a specified numerical method\n\n### Usage\n\n\
``ResourceFunction[\"NumericalMethodFindRoot\"][f, x, method]``\n\tsearches \
for a numerical root of ``f`` as a function of ``x``, using the specified \
method.\n\n``ResourceFunction[\"NumericalMethodFindRoot\"][f, {x, x0}, \
method]``\n\tsearches for a numerical root of ``f``, starting from the point \
``x = x0``.\n\n``ResourceFunction[\"NumericalMethodFindRoot\"][f, {x, x0}, \
method, property]``\n\treturns the specified ``property`` for the numerical \
search.\n\n======\n\n# WFR > NumericalMethodFindRoot > Examples > Basic \
Examples > 3\n\
https://resources.wolframcloud.com/FunctionRepository/resources/\
NumericalMethodFindRoot#1211211167\n\nExamine step information:\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= grid = NumericalMethodFindRoot[x Cos[x], {x, 2}, \"Newton\", \
\"Steps\"]\n\nOut[1]=\n|         |         |                           |\n| \
------- | ------- | ------------------------- |\n| \"steps\" | \"x\"     | \
\"f[x]\"                    |\n| 1       | 2.      | -0.832294                \
 |\n| 2       | 1.62757 | -0.0923469                |\n| 3       | 1.57265 | \
-0.00291944               |\n| 4       | 1.5708  | -3.434685656571718`*^-6   \
|\n| 5       | 1.5708  | -4.7810739535448104`*^-12 |\n| 6       | 1.5708  | \
9.618353468608949`*^-17   |\n| 7       | 1.5708  | 9.618353468608949`*^-17   \
|\n```\n\nThe raw data comprising the grid can be returned by applying \
[Normal](paclet:ref/Normal) :\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= Normal@grid\n\nOut[2]= \
{{\"steps\", \"x\", \"f[x]\"}, {1, 2., -0.832294}, {2, 1.62757, -0.0923469}, \
{3, 1.57265, -0.00291944}, {4, 1.5708, -3.434685656571718`*^-6}, {5, 1.5708, \
-4.7810739535448104`*^-12}, {6, 1.5708, 9.618353468608949`*^-17}, {7, 1.5708, \
9.618353468608949`*^-17}}\n```\nENDRESULT(vhwllfq65)", 
    "Parameters" -> <|
     "query" -> 
      "Wolfram Language root condition polynomial \.10rho BDF method \
numerical stability roots simplify finding roots"|>, "Open" -> False|>,
  Background->None,ExpressionUUID->"01eb22a4-4490-b34c-a905-82f48ab7889d"],
 "\nA simple way to ",
 StyleBox["simplify finding the roots of",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|
    "boxes" -> FormBox["\[Rho]", TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "\\rho", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2681eb79-c4e8-ff4d-b3a1-8eb15de83fcd"],
 " that satisfy the ",
 StyleBox["root condition",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " is to use the fact that for BDF-type methods you usually only need to \
check the polynomial\[CloseCurlyQuote]s roots numerically and then test the \
root-condition criteria directly.\n\nIn Wolfram Language, the main workflow \
is:\n\n1. Build the characteristic polynomial ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{"\[Rho]", "(", "\[Zeta]", ")"}], TraditionalForm], 
     "errors" -> {}, "input" -> "\\rho(\\zeta)", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8b9f5fed-9bfb-cd49-9c04-55231b79656d"],
 "\n2. Find its roots with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
   "NRoots", "paclet:ref/NRoots", 
    "https://reference.wolfram.com/language/ref/NRoots.html"},
   "TextRefLink"]],ExpressionUUID->"c4dbc7a0-9cae-6341-8914-bc469effd678"],
 " or ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
   "NSolve", "paclet:ref/NSolve", 
    "https://reference.wolfram.com/language/ref/NSolve.html"},
   "TextRefLink"]],ExpressionUUID->"6bebcaca-1a97-dd49-9e99-afde8c24e84b"],
 "\n3. Test the root condition: \n",
 Cell[TextData[{
  "   ",
  StyleBox["\[Bullet]",
   FontColor->LightDarkSwitched[
     GrayLevel[0.5], 
     GrayLevel[0.7533742]]],
  " ",
  "all roots satisfy ",
  Cell[BoxData[
   FormBox[
    TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
        RowBox[{"|", "\[Zeta]", "|", "\[LessEqual]", "1"}], TraditionalForm], 
      "errors" -> {}, "input" -> "|\\zeta| \\le 1", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
     "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
   "7ec2bce5-0217-7a4c-ae7f-489dd2b579ea"]
 }], "InlineSubitem",ExpressionUUID->"58839b3b-b328-1b43-93d1-e5f8d8e46817"],
 "\n",
 Cell[TextData[{
  "   ",
  StyleBox["\[Bullet]",
   FontColor->LightDarkSwitched[
     GrayLevel[0.5], 
     GrayLevel[0.7533742]]],
  " ",
  "any root on the unit circle is simple"
 }], "InlineSubitem",ExpressionUUID->"05983e5a-e49d-e84a-a470-1f044cbd80ed"],
 "\n",
 Cell[TextData[{
  "   ",
  StyleBox["\[Bullet]",
   FontColor->LightDarkSwitched[
     GrayLevel[0.5], 
     GrayLevel[0.7533742]]],
  " ",
  "the root at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
   FormBox[
    TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
        RowBox[{"\[Zeta]", "\[LongEqual]", "1"}], TraditionalForm], 
      "errors" -> {}, "input" -> "\\zeta = 1", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
     "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
   "cb823815-141f-5f47-bef5-6f97ad01724b"],
  " is present"
 }], "InlineSubitem",ExpressionUUID->"fed2a3bc-2455-2947-8fd3-72c033ef51c9"],
 "\nFor example: \n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"roots", "=", 
            RowBox[{"z", " ", "/.", " ", 
              RowBox[{"NSolve", "[", 
                RowBox[{
                  RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"rho", "[", "z", "]"}], "==", "0"}], ",", "z"}], 
                "]"}]}]}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"rootConditionQ", "[", 
            RowBox[{"rho_", ",", "z_"}], "]"}], ":=", 
          RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"{", "roots", "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "  ", 
              RowBox[{
                RowBox[{"roots", "=", 
                  RowBox[{"z", " ", "/.", " ", 
                    RowBox[{"NSolve", "[", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"rho", "[", "z", "]"}], "==", "0"}], ",", "z"}], 
                    "]"}]}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "  ", 
                RowBox[{
                  RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"Max", "[", 
                    RowBox[{"Abs", "[", "roots", "]"}], "]"}], "<=", 
                    RowBox[{"1", "+", 
                    RowBox[{"10", "^", 
                    RowBox[{"-", "10"}]}]}]}], "&&", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
                  "  ", 
                  RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"Count", "[", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"Chop", "[", 
                    RowBox[{"roots", "-", "1"}], "]"}], ",", "0"}], "]"}], 
                    ">=", "1"}], "&&", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "  ", 
                  RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"Select", "[", 
                    RowBox[{"roots", ",", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"Abs", "[", "#", "]"}], ">", 
                    RowBox[{"1", "-", 
                    RowBox[{"10", "^", 
                    RowBox[{"-", "10"}]}]}]}], " ", "&"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
                    "True"}], "]"}]}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]}]], 
     "ChatCode", "Input", LanguageCategory -> "Input", ShowAutoStyles -> True,
      ShowStringCharacters -> True, ShowSyntaxStyles -> True, 
     TranslationOptions -> {"Enabled" -> Dynamic[
         AbsoluteCurrentValue[$FrontEnd, {TranslationOptions, "Enabled"}]]}], 
    "Wolfram"},
   "ChatCodeBlockTemplate"]], "ChatCodeBlock",ExpressionUUID->
  "01e7280c-7b80-d249-928a-c984ad160d41"],
 "\nBut a more direct and cleaner test is usually: \n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"rootConditionQ", "[", 
          RowBox[{"rho_", ",", "z_"}], "]"}], ":=", 
        RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"{", 
              RowBox[{"roots", ",", "unitRoots"}], "}"}], ",", 
            "\[IndentingNewLine]", "  ", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"roots", "=", 
                RowBox[{"z", " ", "/.", " ", 
                  RowBox[{"NSolve", "[", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"rho", "[", "z", "]"}], "==", "0"}], ",", "z"}], 
                    "]"}]}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "  ", 
              RowBox[{"unitRoots", "=", 
                RowBox[{"Select", "[", 
                  RowBox[{"roots", ",", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"Abs", "[", "#", "]"}], "<=", 
                    RowBox[{"1", "+", 
                    RowBox[{"10", "^", 
                    RowBox[{"-", "10"}]}]}]}], " ", "&"}]}], "]"}]}], ";", 
              "\[IndentingNewLine]", "  ", 
              RowBox[{
                RowBox[{
                  RowBox[{"Max", "[", 
                    RowBox[{"Abs", "[", "roots", "]"}], "]"}], "<=", 
                  RowBox[{"1", "+", 
                    RowBox[{"10", "^", 
                    RowBox[{"-", "10"}]}]}]}], "&&", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
                "  ", 
                RowBox[{
                  RowBox[{"Count", "[", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"Chop", "[", 
                    RowBox[{"roots", "-", "1"}], "]"}], ",", "0"}], "]"}], 
                  "==", "1"}], "&&", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "  ", 
                RowBox[{"DuplicateFreeQ", "[", 
                  RowBox[{"Chop", "[", "unitRoots", "]"}], "]"}]}]}]}], 
          "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "ChatCode", "Input", 
     LanguageCategory -> "Input", ShowAutoStyles -> True, 
     ShowStringCharacters -> True, ShowSyntaxStyles -> True, 
     TranslationOptions -> {"Enabled" -> Dynamic[
         AbsoluteCurrentValue[$FrontEnd, {TranslationOptions, "Enabled"}]]}], 
    "Wolfram"},
   "ChatCodeBlockTemplate"]], "ChatCodeBlock",ExpressionUUID->
  "ff25e0d6-9960-1c49-bbe9-ec780a790098"],
 "\nIf you want, I can also help you derive the actual ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{"\[Rho]", "(", "\[Zeta]", ")"}], TraditionalForm], 
     "errors" -> {}, "input" -> "\\rho(\\zeta)", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "aa1592f2-ca7f-9b42-994a-a06c30dd96c6"],
 " for ",
 StyleBox["BDF2",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " in the Arevalo\[Dash]S\[ODoubleDot]derlind framework and show how to check \
the root condition symbolically and numerically."
}], "ChatOutput",
 TaggingRules-><|
  "CellToStringData" -> 
   "/doc_search\nWolfram Language root condition polynomial \.10rho BDF \
method numerical stability roots simplify finding roots\n/exec\nRESULT\n# \
NRoots\npaclet:ref/NRoots#22636\n\nNRoots[lhs == rhs, var] yields a \
disjunction of equations which represent numerical approximations to the \
roots of a polynomial equation.\n\n## Details and Options\n\n* ``NRoots`` \
gives several identical equations when roots with multiplicity greater than \
one occur.\n\n* ``NRoots`` has the following options:\n\n|                |   \
        |                                      |\n| -------------- | \
--------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| MaxIterations  | \
Automatic | maximum number of iterations to use  |\n| Method         | \
Automatic | method to use                        |\n| PrecisionGoal  | \
Automatic | the precision sought                 |\n| StepMonitor    | None   \
   | expression to evaluate at each step  |\n\n* Possible settings for the \
``Method`` option include: ``\"Aberth\"``, ``\"CompanionMatrix\"``, and \
``\"JenkinsTraub\"``.\n\n======\n\n# NRoots > Examples > Options > Method\n\
paclet:ref/NRoots#597745998\n\nUse the ``\"Aberth\"`` method, which \
simultaneously approximates all the roots of a univariate polynomial and \
converges cubically in general (but linearly at multiple zeros):\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= Block[{it = 0}, NRoots[z ^ 5 - 10z ^ 4 + 43z ^ 3 - 104z ^ 2 + 150z - \
100 == 0, z, Method -> \"Aberth\", StepMonitor :> Print[\"Iteration: \", \
++it, \", roots:\", Chop[N[z]]]]]\n\nDuring evaluation of In[1]:=\n\
\"Iteration: \"1\", roots:\"{Complex[0.4499646220416188, \
-0.4919108489913469], \n Complex[0.1458910930532221, 1.4349101951891194], \
Complex[2.2413246459073264, 0.9089071956336795], \n \
Complex[-1.3605135801244272, 6.414748849198775], Complex[1.3605135801244264, \
-6.414748849198775]}\n\nDuring evaluation of In[1]:=\n\"Iteration: \"2\", \
roots:\"{1.9999999999999984, Complex[1., 2.], \n Complex[0.9999999999999999, \
-2.0000000000000004], Complex[2.9999999999999982, \n  -0.9999999999999968], \
Complex[3.0000000000000004, 0.9999999999999964]}\n\nOut[1]= z == 1.\
\[VeryThinSpace] - 2. I || z == 1.\[VeryThinSpace] + 2. I || z == 2. || z == \
3.\[VeryThinSpace] - 1. I || z == 3.\[VeryThinSpace] + 1. I\n```\n\n======\n\n\
# NRoots > Examples > Properties & Relations > 3\npaclet:ref/NRoots#430827256\
\n\nCompare with the numerical roots returned by ``NSolve`` :\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= NSolve[1 + 2 x + 3 x ^ 2 + 4 x ^ 3 == 0, x]\n\nOut[1]= {{x -> \
-0.60583}, {x -> -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I}, {x -> -0.0720852 + 0.638327 I}}\n\
```\n\n======\n\n# NRoots > Examples > Scope\npaclet:ref/NRoots#1470975036\n\n\
Find the numerical roots of a polynomial with only real roots:\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= NRoots[x ^ 3 - 5x ^ 2 + 6x - 1 == 0, x]\n\nOut[1]= x == 0.198062 || x \
== 1.55496 || x == 3.24698\n```\n\nFind the numerical roots of a polynomial \
with real and complex roots:\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= NRoots[x ^ 3 - x ^ 2 - x - 1 \
== 0, x]\n\nOut[2]= x == -0.419643 - 0.606291 I || x == -0.419643 + 0.606291 \
I || x == 1.83929\n```\n\nFind the numerical roots of a polynomial with \
multiple roots:\n\n```wl\nIn[3]:= NRoots[(x - 1)(x - 2)^2(x - 3)^3 == 0, x]\n\
\nOut[3]= x == 1. || x == 2. || x == 2. || x == 3. || x == 3. || x == 3.\n```\
\n\n======\n\n# Numerical Operations on Functions > Numerical Root Finding\n\
paclet:tutorial/NumericalOperationsOnFunctions#28899\n\n``NSolve`` gives you \
a general way to find numerical approximations to the solutions of polynomial \
equations. Finding numerical solutions to more general equations, however, \
can be much more difficult, as discussed in [\"Equations in One \
Variable\"](paclet:tutorial/ManipulatingEquationsAndInequalities#16840). \
``FindRoot`` gives you a way to search for a numerical root of a function or \
a numerical solution to an arbitrary equation, or set of equations.\n\n|      \
                                            |                                 \
                                                 |\n| \
------------------------------------------------ | \
------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
-- |\n| FindRoot[f, {x, x0}]                             | search for a \
numerical root of f, starting with x = x0                           |\n| \
FindRoot[lhs == rhs, {x, x0}]                    | search for a numerical \
solution to the equation lhs == rhs, starting with x = x0 |\n| FindRoot[f1, \
f2, \[Ellipsis], {{x, x0}, {y, y0}, \[Ellipsis]}]       |                     \
                                                             |\n|             \
                                     | search for a simultaneous numerical \
root of all the fi                           |\n| FindRoot[{eqn1, eqn2, \
\[Ellipsis]}, {{x, x0}, {y, y0}, \[Ellipsis]}] |                              \
                                                    |\n|                      \
                            | search for a numerical solution to the \
simultaneous equations eqni               |\n\nNumerical root finding.\n\nThe \
curves for $$\\cos (x)$$ and $$x$$ intersect at one point:\n\n```wl\nIn[53]:= \
Plot[{Cos[x], x}, {x, -1, 1}]\n\nOut[53]= <image removed>\n```\n\nThis finds \
a numerical approximation to the value of $$x$$ at which the intersection \
occurs. The ``0`` tells ``FindRoot`` what value of $$x$$ to try first:\n\n\
```wl\nIn[54]:= FindRoot[Cos[x] == x, {x, 0}]\n\nOut[54]= {x -> 0.739085}\n\
```\n\nIn trying to find a solution to your equation, ``FindRoot`` starts at \
the point you specify, and then progressively tries to get closer and closer \
to a solution. Even if your equations have several solutions, ``FindRoot`` \
always returns the first solution it finds. Which solution this is will \
depend on what starting point you chose. If you start sufficiently close to a \
particular solution, ``FindRoot`` will usually return that solution.\n\nThe \
function $$\\sin (x)$$ has an infinite number of roots of the form $$x=n \
\\pi$$. If you start sufficiently close to a particular root, ``FindRoot`` \
will give you that root:\n\n```wl\nIn[55]:= FindRoot[Sin[x], {x, 3}]\n\n\
Out[55]= {x -> 3.14159}\n```\n\nIf you start with $$x=6$$, you get a \
numerical approximation to the root $$x=2\\pi$$ :\n\n```wl\nIn[56]:= \
FindRoot[Sin[x], {x, 6}]\n\nOut[56]= {x -> 6.28319}\n```\n\nIf you want \
``FindRoot`` to search for complex solutions, then you have to give a complex \
starting value:\n\n```wl\nIn[57]:= FindRoot[Sin[x] == 2, {x, I}]\n\nOut[57]= \
{x -> 1.5708 + 1.31696 I}\n```\n\nThis finds a zero of the Riemann zeta \
function:\n\n```wl\nIn[58]:= FindRoot[Zeta[1 / 2 + I t], {t, 12}]\n\nOut[58]= \
{t -> 14.1347 - 2.5483929136770888`*^-15 I}\n```\n\nThis finds a solution to \
a set of simultaneous equations:\n\n```wl\nIn[59]:= FindRoot[{Sin[x] == \
Cos[y], x + y == 1}, {{x, 1}, {y, 1}}]\n\nOut[59]= {x -> -1.85619, y -> \
2.85619}\n```\n\nThe variables used by ``FindRoot`` can have values that are \
lists. This allows you to find roots of functions that take vectors as \
arguments.\n\nThis is a way to solve a linear equation for the variable $$x$$ \
:\n\n```wl\nIn[60]:= FindRoot[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}.x == {5, 6}, {x, {1, 1}}]\n\n\
Out[60]= {x -> {-4., 4.5}}\n```\n\nThis finds a normalized eigenvector $$x$$ \
and eigenvalue $$a$$ :\n\n```wl\nIn[61]:= FindRoot[{{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}.x == a \
x, x.x == 1}, {{x, {1, 1}}, {a, 1}}]\n\nOut[61]= {x -> {0.415974, 0.909377}, \
a -> 5.37228}\n```\n\n======\n\n# NRoots > Examples > Basic Examples\n\
paclet:ref/NRoots#66905662\n\nNumerically find the roots of a polynomial:\n\n\
```wl\nIn[1]:= NRoots[1 + 2x + 3x ^ 2 + 4x ^ 3 == 0, x]\n\nOut[1]= x == \
-0.60583 || x == -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I || x == -0.0720852 + 0.638327 I\n```\
\n\nChange the result to rules:\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= {ToRules[%]}\n\nOut[2]= {{x \
-> -0.60583}, {x -> -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I}, {x -> -0.0720852 + 0.638327 I}}\
\n```\n\nTest the roots:\n\n```wl\nIn[3]:= 1 + 2x + 3x ^ 2 + 4x ^ 3 /. %\n\n\
Out[3]= {1.1102230246251565`*^-16, 1.1102230246251565`*^-16 + 0. I, \
1.1102230246251565`*^-16 + 0. I}\n```\n\n======\n\n# NRoots > Examples > \
Properties & Relations\npaclet:ref/NRoots#1452787437\n\nReturn numerical \
roots using ``NRoots`` :\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:= NRoots[1 + 2 x + 3 x ^ 2 + 4 x ^ 3 \
== 0, x]\n\nOut[1]= x == -0.60583 || x == -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I || x == \
-0.0720852 + 0.638327 I\n\nIn[2]:= {ToRules[%]}\n\nOut[2]= {{x -> -0.60583}, \
{x -> -0.0720852 - 0.638327 I}, {x -> -0.0720852 + 0.638327 I}}\n```\n\n\
======\n\n# FindRoot > Examples > Basic Examples > 2\n\
paclet:ref/FindRoot#24146\n\nFind a solution to $$\\cos (x)=x$$ near $$x=0$$ \
:\n\n```wl\nIn[1]:= FindRoot[Cos[x] == x, {x, 0}]\n\nOut[1]= {x -> 0.739085}\n\
```\n\n======\n\n# WFR > NumericalMethodFindRoot > Description\n\
https://resources.wolframcloud.com/FunctionRepository/resources/\
NumericalMethodFindRoot#152795533\n\nFind the root of an equation or number \
using a specified numerical method\n\n### Usage\n\n\
``ResourceFunction[\"NumericalMethodFindRoot\"][f, x, method]``\n\tsearches \
for a numerical root of ``f`` as a function of ``x``, using the specified \
method.\n\n``ResourceFunction[\"NumericalMethodFindRoot\"][f, {x, x0}, \
method]``\n\tsearches for a numerical root of ``f``, starting from the point \
``x = x0``.\n\n``ResourceFunction[\"NumericalMethodFindRoot\"][f, {x, x0}, \
method, property]``\n\treturns the specified ``property`` for the numerical \
search.\n\n======\n\n# WFR > NumericalMethodFindRoot > Examples > Basic \
Examples > 3\n\
https://resources.wolframcloud.com/FunctionRepository/resources/\
NumericalMethodFindRoot#1211211167\n\nExamine step information:\n\n```wl\n\
In[1]:= grid = NumericalMethodFindRoot[x Cos[x], {x, 2}, \"Newton\", \
\"Steps\"]\n\nOut[1]=\n|         |         |                           |\n| \
------- | ------- | ------------------------- |\n| \"steps\" | \"x\"     | \
\"f[x]\"                    |\n| 1       | 2.      | -0.832294                \
 |\n| 2       | 1.62757 | -0.0923469                |\n| 3       | 1.57265 | \
-0.00291944               |\n| 4       | 1.5708  | -3.434685656571718`*^-6   \
|\n| 5       | 1.5708  | -4.7810739535448104`*^-12 |\n| 6       | 1.5708  | \
9.618353468608949`*^-17   |\n| 7       | 1.5708  | 9.618353468608949`*^-17   \
|\n```\n\nThe raw data comprising the grid can be returned by applying \
[Normal](paclet:ref/Normal) :\n\n```wl\nIn[2]:= Normal@grid\n\nOut[2]= \
{{\"steps\", \"x\", \"f[x]\"}, {1, 2., -0.832294}, {2, 1.62757, -0.0923469}, \
{3, 1.57265, -0.00291944}, {4, 1.5708, -3.434685656571718`*^-6}, {5, 1.5708, \
-4.7810739535448104`*^-12}, {6, 1.5708, 9.618353468608949`*^-17}, {7, 1.5708, \
9.618353468608949`*^-17}}\n```\nENDRESULT(vhwllfq65)\n\nA simple way to \
**simplify finding the roots of** $$\\rho$$ that satisfy the **root \
condition** is to use the fact that for BDF-type methods you usually only \
need to check the polynomial\[CloseCurlyQuote]s roots numerically and then \
test the root-condition criteria directly.\n\nIn Wolfram Language, the main \
workflow is:\n\n1. Build the characteristic polynomial $$\\rho(\\zeta)$$\n2. \
Find its roots with [NRoots](paclet:ref/NRoots) or \
[NSolve](paclet:ref/NSolve)\n3. Test the root condition:\n   - all roots \
satisfy $$|\\zeta| \\le 1$$\n   - any root on the unit circle is simple\n   - \
the root at $$\\zeta = 1$$ is present\n\nFor example:\n\n```wl\nroots = z /. \
NSolve[rho[z] == 0, z];\n\nrootConditionQ[rho_, z_] := Module[{roots},\n  \
roots = z /. NSolve[rho[z] == 0, z];\n  Max[Abs[roots]] <= 1 + 10^-10 &&\n  \
Count[Chop[roots - 1], 0] >= 1 &&\n  AllTrue[Select[roots, Abs[#] > 1 - \
10^-10 &], True]\n]\n```\n\nBut a more direct and cleaner test is usually:\n\n\
```wl\nrootConditionQ[rho_, z_] := Module[{roots, unitRoots},\n  roots = z /. \
NSolve[rho[z] == 0, z];\n  unitRoots = Select[roots, Abs[#] <= 1 + 10^-10 &];\
\n  Max[Abs[roots]] <= 1 + 10^-10 &&\n  Count[Chop[roots - 1], 0] == 1 &&\n  \
DuplicateFreeQ[Chop[unitRoots]]\n]\n```\n\nIf you want, I can also help you \
derive the actual $$\\rho(\\zeta)$$ for **BDF2** in the Arevalo\[Dash]S\
\[ODoubleDot]derlind framework and show how to check the root condition \
symbolically and numerically.", "ChatData" -> 
